"""Prepared reference solution for the functional ``aten::_foreach_log2``.

Reference-as-solution phase only. The project's own formal injection path
(``--override foreach_log2:<this file>:run`` -> ``flag_gems.cli_override.
parse_override_spec`` -> ``flag_gems.dynamic_registry.DynamicOpOverride.
override_from_file``) loads this module and assigns ``run`` to
``flag_gems.foreach_log2``. That attribute is exactly what the frozen dev
correctness test calls (``tests/test_foreach_log2.py::_gems_op`` ->
``flag_gems.foreach_log2(values)``) and what the benchmark resolves through
``Benchmark._candidate_call()`` -> ``Config.override_registry.
get_override("foreach_log2")``, so no other entrypoint or bypass is used.

The oracle is the native ATen op, i.e. the same callable the frozen test uses
for its expected value (``torch.ops.aten._foreach_log2``), not the overridden
FlagGems export: this module imports nothing from ``flag_gems`` and therefore
cannot call the current candidate as its own oracle.

Contract preserved from the frozen definition ``definitions/foreach_log2.json``
and from the operator's ATen schema ``aten::_foreach_log2(Tensor[] self) ->
Tensor[]``:

* one newly allocated output tensor per input element, in input order;
* no input mutation and no aliasing of any input storage
  (``effects.mutates == []``, ``effects.returns_alias_of == {}``);
* dtype/device behaviour is ATen's own: floating dtypes keep their dtype,
  integral inputs follow ATen's promotion policy, complex inputs follow the
  backend's capability;
* an empty TensorList is rejected with ``RuntimeError`` exactly as ATen does
  (``tests/test_foreach_log2.py::test_foreach_log2_rejects_empty_list``).
"""

import torch


def run(values):
    """Apply ``aten::_foreach_log2`` to a TensorList and return the outputs."""
    return torch.ops.aten._foreach_log2(list(values))
